WEBVTT

00:04.000 --> 00:04.360
All right.

00:04.360 --> 00:08.480
So now we're going to learn how to define a window inside the over clause.

00:08.520 --> 00:11.480
The first part that we can define is the partition p.

00:11.520 --> 00:14.120
So for example here we have partition p category.

00:14.120 --> 00:15.720
We have to define the dimension.

00:15.720 --> 00:18.320
It's very similar to the groupby and other wording.

00:18.640 --> 00:21.120
So the first part is going to be the partition clause.

00:21.160 --> 00:26.320
What it's going to do it's going to divide the entire data sets into groups.

00:26.320 --> 00:29.000
Or you can call it windows partitions.

00:29.160 --> 00:32.400
So here we tell SQL how to divide our data.

00:32.560 --> 00:33.720
And here we have two options.

00:33.720 --> 00:34.720
Let me just show you.

00:34.920 --> 00:38.080
So if we don't use anything so we have it empty.

00:38.120 --> 00:41.400
You see over and partition by is not used.

00:41.440 --> 00:42.240
What can happen.

00:42.280 --> 00:46.960
SQL gonna use the entire data in order to do the calculations.

00:47.080 --> 00:51.600
So the whole data, the entire data can be counted as one window.

00:51.600 --> 00:54.080
So we are telling SQL don't divide anything.

00:54.120 --> 00:55.280
Leave it as it is.

00:55.560 --> 00:59.040
The second option that we have is to divide the data by partition p.

00:59.080 --> 01:03.450
So we define the window like this partition partitioned by products for example.

01:03.450 --> 01:09.250
So SQL going to go and divide the entire data into different windows for example here to windows and

01:09.250 --> 01:14.330
here this time the calculation the sum of sales will not apply on the entire data set.

01:14.370 --> 01:18.570
This time it's going to be applied on the different windows individually.

01:18.570 --> 01:24.410
So we're going to find the sum of sales for window one separately from the total sales of window two.

01:24.570 --> 01:24.810
All right.

01:24.810 --> 01:26.530
So now we have this very simple example.

01:26.530 --> 01:29.770
We have here three fields the month product sales.

01:29.810 --> 01:31.450
They are really easy informations.

01:31.450 --> 01:34.290
And now we have the following SQL window function.

01:34.290 --> 01:36.450
So we have sum of sales.

01:36.650 --> 01:40.210
And inside the over clause we are not using anything.

01:40.210 --> 01:42.410
So we are not using partition by.

01:42.610 --> 01:44.490
So how is SQL going to define the window.

01:44.490 --> 01:47.890
Now SQL is going to say okay I don't have to divide anything.

01:48.130 --> 01:51.050
The entire data set is one window.

01:51.050 --> 01:55.170
So SQL is going to go over here and say the whole thing is one window.

01:55.170 --> 01:57.770
So there is no partitions, there is nothing.

01:57.770 --> 01:59.650
We have only one window.

01:59.650 --> 02:01.890
So the entire data can be aggregated.

02:01.890 --> 02:03.060
So this is what happened.

02:03.060 --> 02:08.660
If you don't use partition B and you leave the other clause empty, the entire data is one window.

02:08.700 --> 02:08.980
All right.

02:08.980 --> 02:10.660
So now let's move to the next example.

02:10.660 --> 02:12.260
We don't want to have only one window.

02:12.260 --> 02:14.140
We would like to have multiple windows.

02:14.140 --> 02:16.500
So we have to divide the data by something.

02:16.500 --> 02:21.980
So in the over clause we're going to define the window like the following partition by month.

02:21.980 --> 02:23.100
So it's not empty.

02:23.420 --> 02:26.780
We are now dividing the data by the filled month.

02:26.940 --> 02:31.340
So the values inside this column are going to divide the data sets.

02:31.340 --> 02:34.660
So here we have two months January and February.

02:34.660 --> 02:38.940
So what's going to do SQL going to go and divide the data into two sets.

02:38.980 --> 02:41.740
The first window going to be this one of January.

02:41.740 --> 02:47.460
So we have the first window going to make it smaller and the second window going to be the February.

02:47.460 --> 02:50.700
So it's going to be two windows inside our data.

02:50.700 --> 02:54.580
And the calculation is going to be happening on each window separately.

02:54.580 --> 03:01.140
So here as you can see we are using the month in order to divide our data sets into two windows one

03:01.140 --> 03:04.870
window for January and another window for that February.

03:04.870 --> 03:08.910
So now let's have a quick overview about the options that we have with the partition by the first option.

03:08.910 --> 03:11.110
As we learned we can just skip it.

03:11.110 --> 03:14.990
So without partition by for example here total sales across all rows.

03:15.030 --> 03:18.070
And here we don't find anything inside the SQL.

03:18.110 --> 03:23.550
The second option we can use one field one column for example partition by products.

03:23.550 --> 03:25.710
So we are using one dimension.

03:25.710 --> 03:27.150
But we can go and mix stuff.

03:27.150 --> 03:32.750
We can use multiple columns or multiple dimensions in the partition by for example here partition by

03:32.790 --> 03:34.470
product and order status.

03:34.470 --> 03:39.630
So here with the partition by, we can define a list of dimensions that could be used in order to divide

03:39.630 --> 03:40.390
our data.

03:40.390 --> 03:47.150
So in this example we are saying find the total sales for each combination of products and order status.

03:47.150 --> 03:50.350
So those are the different options on how to work with the partition by.

03:50.510 --> 03:53.550
So now let's have this overview again for all functions.

03:53.830 --> 03:57.310
The partition by for all those functions is optional.

03:57.310 --> 04:01.830
So if you don't use the partition by in all those functions you will not get any errors.

04:01.830 --> 04:05.800
So now let's go back to SQL in order to start practicing with this clause.

04:05.840 --> 04:07.360
Okay, so now we have the following task.

04:07.400 --> 04:09.800
Find the total sales across all orders.

04:09.800 --> 04:14.240
And we have to provide additional informations like the order ID and the order date.

04:14.560 --> 04:16.680
So let's go and solve it step by step.

04:16.880 --> 04:18.840
First I would like to provide the details.

04:18.840 --> 04:25.480
So I'm going to select the order ID and the order dates from the table sales orders.

04:25.760 --> 04:27.480
And next we're going to work with the aggregations.

04:27.480 --> 04:30.560
So we need to find the total sales across all orders.

04:30.600 --> 04:35.600
Again since we have here details and aggregations, we cannot use groupby we have to use the window

04:35.600 --> 04:36.360
function.

04:36.360 --> 04:40.040
So we're going to go use the function sum for sales.

04:40.040 --> 04:44.040
And now we have to tell SQL we are working with window functions.

04:44.040 --> 04:46.000
That's why we're going to use the over clause.

04:46.040 --> 04:49.200
And now the next step we have to think about defining the window.

04:49.440 --> 04:50.560
So let's check the task.

04:50.600 --> 04:53.320
It says total sales across all orders.

04:53.600 --> 04:59.560
So that means we don't have to partition or divide the data sets into like chunks or partitions.

04:59.560 --> 05:03.400
We have to leave it as it is, like the whole data is going to be one window.

05:03.440 --> 05:05.660
And that's why we don't use partition p pi.

05:05.700 --> 05:07.900
Inside the definition, we're going to leave it empty.

05:07.940 --> 05:09.180
Let's go now and give it a name.

05:09.180 --> 05:11.060
It's going to be the total sales.

05:11.180 --> 05:12.540
Let's go and execute this.

05:12.540 --> 05:13.580
And now add the results.

05:13.580 --> 05:16.580
As you can see we have all the orders, all the details.

05:16.580 --> 05:20.380
And as well we have the total sales across all orders.

05:20.380 --> 05:21.900
So with that we have solved the tasks.

05:21.900 --> 05:25.260
We have the total sales and as well some details about the order.

05:25.300 --> 05:25.620
All right.

05:25.620 --> 05:27.100
So now let's move to the next task.

05:27.140 --> 05:28.580
It's going to be very similar.

05:28.580 --> 05:31.900
So it says find the total sales for each product.

05:31.900 --> 05:35.660
And we have to provide additional information like the order ID and the order dates.

05:35.660 --> 05:37.580
So it's going to be very similar task.

05:37.580 --> 05:42.860
But this time we have to divide the entire data into windows.

05:43.020 --> 05:47.460
And that's going to be by the products since we are saying total sales for each product.

05:47.620 --> 05:50.500
So this time we have to go and divide the data.

05:50.500 --> 05:53.380
So we're going to define the window like this partition by.

05:53.700 --> 05:56.620
And we can use the dimension products ID.

05:56.660 --> 05:58.700
Let's go and execute this.

05:58.700 --> 06:03.340
So now you can see in the total sales we don't have anymore the total sales of the whole data.

06:03.340 --> 06:04.660
